    parser -- generates a new kant parse for every rule its argument touches.

    parser(rule) walks the rule and everything below it, generates a parse body
    for each, compiles it, and closes by parsing a specimen through the rule so
    the run reports an answer rather than a count. parser(Start) is the POP.

    Three phases, two gates. Generation always runs. Activation binds the
    generated body as the rule's parse method and is gated by activating.
    Compilation is gated by compiling. A measuring run turns one or both off; a
    verifying run leaves both on, because a parse method that is never bound is
    never exercised.

    Both gates are declared fields and both are tested against a value, never
    bare. A bare test on a declared field asks whether it exists, which is
    always true.

    The witness line reports whether compile actually ran on the rule, read off
    the hasNewParsE flag that compile sets as its last act. It exists because
    the answer alone cannot tell the two gate positions apart: a body that was
    never explicitly compiled still parses, because bodies compile lazily at
    first call. So the run prints the answer AND who produced it.

